**Original question.**

Describe and sketch from microscopical examination the object marked B.

=== Solution

TODO: The microscopic object B and its image are absent; its specific description and sketch cannot be supplied.

**The description and sketch of B remain undetermined.** An actual <microscopy> would record the preparation, magnification, structural arrangement and the distinguishing features observed. A drawing would show those observed relationships with a stated scale or magnification; a generic diagram cannot substitute for the absent object.

== 4
{scope}

**Original question.**

Describe the morbid product marked C.

=== Solution

TODO: Morbid product C is not identified or illustrated; its specimen-specific description cannot be supplied.

**The description of C remains undetermined.** A specimen account would document its provenance if known, dimensions, form, surface and cut-surface appearances, consistency and relation to the tissue of origin. Classification would follow the observed evidence; the label C does not establish a tissue, lesion or composition.
